==English==

===Etymology===
Contraction of ''[[line of battle ship]]'', a warship large enough to be in the ''line of battle''. 
===Noun===
'''[[ship]] of the [[line]]'''

# {{nautical}}{{military}} a [[large]] [[square-rigged]] [[warship]] large enough to have a place in the [[line of battle]]. with up to 140 [[gun]]s on at least two [[deck]]s. A [[capital ship]] from the [[age]] of [[sail]], superior to a [[frigate]]; usually, a [[seventy-four]], or [[three-decker]].  (Totten). 

The OED records the terms ''line of battle ship'', '' battleship'', ''ship of the line'' and ''liner'' being used interchangeably up through the 19th century. Modern usage uses ''ship of the line'' for a wooden line of battle ship from the age of sail; ''battleship'' for an armoured and motorized warship of the late 19th and mid 20th centuries.  

